---
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import Icon from "@/components/Icon.astro";
import { site } from "@/data/site";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/url";
---

<Base
  title="Chess Vision"
  description="A computer vision pipeline that photographs a physical chessboard and reconstructs the full board position using three cascaded neural networks."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 pt-36 pb-24 sm:px-12 md:px-24">
    <a
      href={withBase("/#projects")}
      class="font-mono text-muted hover:text-accent inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-xs transition-colors"
    >
      <Icon name="arrow" size={14} class="rotate-180" /> back to portfolio
    </a>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="mt-8 mb-12" data-reveal>
      <p class="eyebrow">EE 5271 · University of Minnesota · Spring 2025</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-semibold sm:text-4xl">Chess Vision</h1>
      <p class="text-muted mt-2 text-lg">Automated Chessboard Position Reconstruction via Computer Vision and Neural Networks</p>
      <p class="text-body mt-3 text-sm">
        Mitchell Salzman
      </p>
      <p class="text-muted mt-1 text-xs">
        Course: Robot Vision (EE 5271)
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Architecture Diagram -->
    <section class="mb-14" data-reveal>
      <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-white">
        <img
          src={withBase("/media/chess-architecture.svg")}
          alt="Chess Vision architecture: photo of board through computer vision preprocessing and three cascaded neural networks to FEN output"
          class="block w-full p-4"
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The Problem -->
    <section class="mb-14" data-reveal>
      <h2 class="text-heading mb-4 text-xl font-semibold">The Problem</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
        Digitizing a physical chess position for analysis, online play, or record-keeping
        means manually entering every piece on a digital board. For casual players recording a
        game or coaches reviewing a student's position, this is tedious and error-prone. A
        system that can photograph any board from any angle and reconstruct the position
        automatically would make that translation instant.
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-4">
        <div class="rounded-lg border-2 border-[var(--color-accent)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-accent text-lg font-bold">3</p>
          <p class="text-accent mt-1 text-xs font-medium">Neural networks</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-heading text-lg font-bold">3,328</p>
          <p class="text-muted mt-1 text-xs">Training images</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-heading text-lg font-bold">64</p>
          <p class="text-muted mt-1 text-xs">Squares extracted</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-4 text-center">
          <p class="text-heading text-lg font-bold">FEN</p>
          <p class="text-muted mt-1 text-xs">Standard output</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Pipeline Overview -->
    <section class="mb-14" data-reveal>
      <h2 class="text-heading mb-4 text-xl font-semibold">Pipeline</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
        The system chains classical computer vision with deep learning. The CV stage handles
        geometry: finding the board, correcting perspective, and extracting individual squares.
        The neural network stage handles semantics, deciding what's on each square.
      </p>

      <div class="mb-8 rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-6">
        <h3 class="text-heading mb-4 text-base font-semibold">Four-Stage Pipeline</h3>
        <div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
          <div>
            <p class="text-accent text-sm font-semibold">1. Edge Detection</p>
            <p class="text-body mt-1 text-[0.85rem]">
              Canny edge detection followed by a Hough transform extracts up to 45 dominant
              lines from the board image.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-accent text-sm font-semibold">2. Line Clustering</p>
            <p class="text-body mt-1 text-[0.85rem]">
              Lines are separated into two directional clusters by theta, pruned by deviation
              from the cluster mean, then mean-clustered into 9 lines per direction.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-accent text-sm font-semibold">3. Homography</p>
            <p class="text-body mt-1 text-[0.85rem]">
              The 81 line intersections define the grid. A projective homography maps the
              perspective-distorted board to a rectified square, producing 64 cropped images.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-accent text-sm font-semibold">4. Classification</p>
            <p class="text-body mt-1 text-[0.85rem]">
              Three cascaded neural networks classify each 48x48 square: occupied or empty,
              black or white, and which piece type.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Computer Vision -->
    <section class="mb-14" data-reveal>
      <h2 class="text-heading mb-4 text-xl font-semibold">Computer Vision</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
        The first half of the pipeline is pure geometry. The goal is to take a photograph of a
        chessboard from an arbitrary camera angle and produce 64 rectified, uniformly-sized
        square images ready for classification.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)]">
          <img
            src={withBase("/media/chess-1-lines.png")}
            alt="Chessboard with detected Hough lines (green and blue) and intersection points (red dots)"
            class="w-full"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <p class="text-muted bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] py-2 text-center text-xs">
            Hough lines (green/blue) and 81 intersection points (red) detected on the board
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)]">
          <img
            src={withBase("/media/chess-2-squares.png")}
            alt="8x8 grid of extracted and rectified individual square images"
            class="w-full"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <p class="text-muted bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] py-2 text-center text-xs">
            64 individual squares extracted after homography correction
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-6">
          <h3 class="text-heading text-base font-semibold">Hough Transform & Line Clustering</h3>
          <p class="text-body mt-2 text-[0.9rem] leading-relaxed">
            After Canny edge detection, the Hough transform produces candidate lines in
            (&theta;, &rho;) space. Lines are split into two directional clusters by their
            average theta, outliers beyond &plusmn;0.2 rad of the cluster mean are pruned, and
            points are projected onto a best-fit line. Mean clustering with an adaptive radius
            consolidates nearby lines into exactly 9 per direction, the board's gridlines.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-6">
          <h3 class="text-heading text-base font-semibold">Projective Homography</h3>
          <p class="text-body mt-2 text-[0.9rem] leading-relaxed">
            The 9&times;9 = 81 intersections define the board grid. Four corner points anchor a
            homography that warps the perspective-distorted image to a rectified square. The
            warped intersection coordinates then define exact crop boundaries for each of the
            64 squares, which are resized to 48&times;48&times;3 for the neural networks.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Neural Networks -->
    <section class="mb-14" data-reveal>
      <h2 class="text-heading mb-4 text-xl font-semibold">Neural Network Cascade</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
        Rather than a single network solving all three questions at once, the system uses three
        specialized classifiers in cascade. Each network is a PyTorch model imported into MATLAB,
        taking 48&times;48&times;3 RGB images as input.
      </p>

      <div class="mb-8 space-y-4">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-6">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
            <div class="rounded-lg bg-[var(--color-accent)] px-3 py-1 text-sm font-bold text-white shrink-0">1</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-heading text-base font-semibold">blankNet: Occupancy</h3>
              <p class="text-body mt-2 text-[0.9rem] leading-relaxed">
                Binary classifier: is this square occupied or empty? Trained on 3,328 images
                (1,664 occupied + 1,664 blank). This gates the downstream networks; empty squares
                skip color and piece classification entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-6">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
            <div class="rounded-lg bg-[var(--color-accent)] px-3 py-1 text-sm font-bold text-white shrink-0">2</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-heading text-base font-semibold">colorNet: Piece Color</h3>
              <p class="text-body mt-2 text-[0.9rem] leading-relaxed">
                Binary classifier: is the piece black or white? Trained on 1,664 images
                (832 per color). Only runs on squares that blankNet classified as occupied.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-6">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
            <div class="rounded-lg bg-[var(--color-accent)] px-3 py-1 text-sm font-bold text-white shrink-0">3</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-heading text-base font-semibold">pieceNet: Piece Type</h3>
              <p class="text-body mt-2 text-[0.9rem] leading-relaxed">
                Six-class classifier: pawn, bishop, knight, rook, queen, or king. Trained on
                1,664 occupied images across all piece types. Runs in parallel with colorNet
                on occupied squares.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Training Data -->
    <section class="mb-14" data-reveal>
      <h2 class="text-heading mb-4 text-xl font-semibold">Training Data</h2>
      <div class="grid items-start gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div>
          <p class="text-body mb-6 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
            The training set was built by photographing chess positions from multiple angles and
            extracting individual square images through the same CV pipeline used at inference.
            This ensures the networks see the same perspective-corrected, cropped inputs during
            training and deployment.
          </p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4">
            <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-4 text-center">
              <p class="text-accent text-lg font-bold">1,664</p>
              <p class="text-muted mt-1 text-xs">Blank squares</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-4 text-center">
              <p class="text-accent text-lg font-bold">1,664</p>
              <p class="text-muted mt-1 text-xs">Occupied squares</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-4 text-center">
              <p class="text-heading text-lg font-bold">832</p>
              <p class="text-muted mt-1 text-xs">Black pieces</p>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-4 text-center">
              <p class="text-heading text-lg font-bold">832</p>
              <p class="text-muted mt-1 text-xs">White pieces</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)]">
          <img
            src={withBase("/media/chess-3-training.png")}
            alt="Training data breakdown: 1,664 blank squares, 832 black pieces, 832 white pieces across 6 piece types"
            class="w-full"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <p class="text-muted bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] py-2 text-center text-xs">
            Training data distribution: 3,328 total images across blank, black, and white classes with 6 piece types
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Output -->
    <section class="mb-14" data-reveal>
      <h2 class="text-heading mb-4 text-xl font-semibold">Output</h2>
      <p class="text-body mb-6 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
        The three networks' predictions are assembled into an 8&times;8 board array. Uppercase
        letters represent white pieces and lowercase represent black (standard chess notation).
        The array is converted to a FEN string, the universal format for encoding chess
        positions, and loaded into a ChessMaster GUI for visualization and play.
      </p>

      <div class="grid items-start gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-6">
          <h3 class="text-heading mb-3 text-base font-semibold">FEN Encoding</h3>
          <dl class="text-body space-y-3 text-[0.9rem]">
            <div>
              <dt class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-heading)] inline">Piece map:</dt>
              <dd class="inline"> p=pawn, b=bishop, n=knight, r=rook, q=queen, k=king</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-heading)] inline">Color:</dt>
              <dd class="inline"> uppercase = white, lowercase = black</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-heading)] inline">Empty squares:</dt>
              <dd class="inline"> consecutive blanks encoded as a digit (e.g. "3" = three empty)</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-heading)] inline">Metadata:</dt>
              <dd class="inline"> active color, castling rights, en passant, move clocks</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)]">
          <img
            src={withBase("/media/chess-4-output.png")}
            alt="Reconstructed chess position rendered in ChessMaster GUI"
            class="w-full"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <p class="text-muted bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] py-2 text-center text-xs">
            Final reconstructed position rendered in ChessMaster from the generated FEN string
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Key Insight -->
    <section class="mb-14" data-reveal>
      <h2 class="text-heading mb-4 text-xl font-semibold">Key Insight</h2>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-line)] bg-[var(--color-bg-raised)] p-6">
        <p class="text-body text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed">
          Splitting the classification into three specialized networks instead of one monolithic
          classifier keeps each problem simple: binary for occupancy, binary for color, and a
          modest six-class problem for piece type. The cascade also saves compute: empty squares
          (typically 32+ of the 64) skip two of three networks entirely. And by sharing the same
          CV front end for both training and inference, the networks never see a domain gap
          between training crops and live inputs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Tech chips -->
    <div class="border-line flex flex-wrap gap-3 border-t pt-8" data-reveal>
      {["MATLAB", "PyTorch", "Computer Vision", "Hough Transform", "Homography", "CNN", "Image Classification"].map(t => (
        <span class="chip">{t}</span>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
